comment_text:
Xers (kids who grew up mostly in the 80s) had a similar experience. I'd say a smaller percentage of us maybe figured out the computer stuff you mentioned - although many of us got into that when we were college-aged (in the 90s). And we didn't have the musical acts you mentioned but had similar excitement with MTV and the bands of our era. I think the huge takeaway is that the 80s and 90s had a magical level of shared zeitgeist that did not exist before or after - a level of entertainment and stuff for kids that surpassed prior decades - but also prior to streaming digital content that gives each kid or group a different experience from each other. When you had the same content as the kid across town, you grew up with this thing you could talk about (and now reminisce about) and bond over. ... I will add though, the 90s were indeed a great time to be young adults too - driving our first cars when gas was only $1/gallon - using car CD players with shock plates and adapters to play through the tape deck - just going anywhere we wanted at all hours, exploring our world, getting our first apartments back when rent was still cheap enough you could do that with a low-paying job that left plenty of free hours to enjoy the things you mentioned. Good stuff. It was indeed a magical time. I'd say we didn't know how good we had it, except some of us did and enjoyed the heck out of it! (We just didn't realize how severely the good times would disappear a decade or two later.
)
1
